he Lions are 8-2 through week 11 of the 2026 season and have outscored opponents 288 to 220. The offense has been carrying the team, ranking 4th in EPA per play while the defense sits 24th in EPA allowed. What sets them apart is the highest under center rate in the league.","offense":"The Lions have one of the most efficient offenses in the league so far, ranking in the top eight in all five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the highest under center rate and the lowest shotgun rate in the league. Jared Goff has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Amon-Ra St. Brown, who has drawn 29% of the targets.","defense":"The Lions defense has been well below average by EPA allowed per play so far. It has been much better against the run than the pass, ranking 8th and 25th. Aidan Hutchinson has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is D.J. Wonnum, who has 7 sacks while playing only 58% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is an eight man box rate that ranks 7th highest in the league.","special":"The Lions have been one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 50% of their short-yardage chances so far. Their kickers have made 89% of field goals, which is a little above average. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way. Their receivers were among the oldest in the league.","players":"Jared Goff has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 2,716 yards. Amon-Ra St. Brown has been the clear top target with 29% of the looks and 792 receiving yards. Jahmyr Gibbs has handled most of the work in the backfield with 84% of the carries. On defense, D.J. Wonnum leads the team with 7 sacks and Chuck Clark has picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Drew Petzing is calling the offense and Kelvin Sheppard is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2023 Raiders and the 2023 Texans under Bobby Slowik. Their defense looks most like the 2024 Dolphins and the 2023 Packers.","trends":"The offense went from 7th in EPA per play in 2025 to 4th so far this season, a modest improvement. The defense moved from 14th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 24th so far this season, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was number of defenders in the box, which went from 6.5 to 6.33.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}